// =============================================================================
// Gateway operations — throttling, failures, and who is being hurt by them
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE: Log Analytics workspace log-provdemo-... , table
//        ApiManagementGatewayLogs (fed by the diagnostic setting in
//        Session 1/infra/01-platform.bicep).
//
// The token metrics answer "what did it cost". These answer "is it healthy, and
// for whom is it not". Both are needed: a platform that is cheap and unreliable
// gets abandoned just as fast as one that is reliable and expensive.
// =============================================================================

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Throttling, split by WHO threw it
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The most important distinction in this whole file.
//
//   GATEWAY 429  = a Providence policy stopped the caller. Working as intended.
//                  The team has hit its own budget. Nobody else is affected.
//
//   BACKEND 429  = Foundry stopped us. Shared capacity is exhausted. This is a
//                  platform problem and it affects every team on that
//                  deployment, including ones that are well behaved.
//
// Conflating the two is how a capacity incident gets misdiagnosed as an app
// team problem for two days.
ApiManagementGatewayLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where ResponseCode == 429 or BackendResponseCode == 429
| extend Source = case(
    BackendResponseCode == 429, "backend (Foundry capacity)",
    "gateway (Providence policy)")
| summarize Throttles = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 15m), Source, ApiId, ProductId
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Error budget by team and API
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ApiManagementGatewayLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | summarize
//     Total   = count(),
//     Errors  = countif(ResponseCode >= 500),
//     Client  = countif(ResponseCode >= 400 and ResponseCode < 500),
//     P95Ms   = percentile(TotalTime, 95)
//   by ProductId, ApiId
// | extend ErrorRatePct = round(100.0 * Errors / Total, 2)
// | order by ErrorRatePct desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Content safety rejections — a leading indicator, not just a control
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A sudden spike in 403s from one application is worth a conversation. It is
// usually one of three things, and they need very different responses:
//   * a genuine misuse attempt                       -> security
//   * a legitimate clinical use case tripping a low  -> tune the threshold for
//     threshold (injury descriptions, self-harm         that product; do not
//     screening, substance-abuse notes)                 tune it globally
//   * a prompt template that changed and now looks   -> app team
//     like an injection
//
// ApiManagementGatewayLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where ResponseCode == 403
// | summarize Rejections = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 1h), ProductId, ApiId
// | render timechart

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Agent egress audit — which agent touched which internal system
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The query a security review will ask for, and the one that has no answer at
// all if agents call internal APIs directly instead of through the gateway.
//
// ApiManagementGatewayLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where ApiId == "clinical-tools"
// | project TimeGenerated, OperationId, ResponseCode, CallerIpAddress,
//           AgentAppId = tostring(parse_json(tostring(RequestHeaders))["x-prov-agent-app-id"]),
//           CorrelationId = tostring(parse_json(tostring(RequestHeaders))["x-prov-correlation-id"])
// | order by TimeGenerated desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Billing reconciliation — gateway tokens vs. invoice
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Expect a gap. Chase it only if it is growing, and know the four usual causes
// before you start:
//
//   1. SAMPLING. If the APIM diagnostic sampling percentage is below 100, the
//      gateway under-reports by construction. Check this first, every time.
//   2. STREAMING. Streamed responses do not always carry a usage block; the
//      llm-* policies handle this, older azure-openai-* ones may not.
//   3. BYPASS. Traffic that did not go through the gateway at all. This is the
//      one that matters: it means the platform has a hole. Compare the gateway
//      total against the Foundry resource's own metric.
//   4. NON-CHAT WORKLOADS. Embeddings, transcription, image generation and
//      realtime bill on different meters and may not be token-shaped.
//
// Run this beside Cost Management filtered to the Foundry resource. Both
// tables live in the same workspace, so no cross-resource app() call is needed:
//
// AppMetrics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
// | where Name == "Total Tokens"
// | extend Model = tostring(Properties.Model)
// | summarize GatewayTokens = sum(Sum) by Model, bin(TimeGenerated, 1d)
// | order by TimeGenerated desc
//
// Note the table name. This is workspace-based App Insights, so it is
// AppMetrics / Properties / Sum, NOT customMetrics / customDimensions /
// valueSum. See the header of 01-token-attribution.kql — reading the wrong
// table returns an empty result with no error and looks like a broken policy.
